//
// PROBE: what converts to a STRING, and what a container leaves out.
//
// `println`, `print`, `display` and `convert.to_string` all ask the same
// question of a value — `conv(STRING)` — and F167 found Bund2 answering it
// differently for five kinds. The refusals end a program, so they are
// unit-tested in `crates/bund2-stdlib/src/convert.rs`. What is here is what
// prints.
//
// **A LAMBDA is a list's rendering behind the word `lambda`**
// (`reference/rust_dynamic/src/conv.rs`, `value_lambda_conversion`). A word
// inside it is a CALL and renders `F(name)`.
{ 1 } println
lambda println
{ 1 "a" dup } println
{ { 1 } 2 } println

// The same text through the conversion word, and inside the two containers.
{ 1 } convert.to_string println
[ 1 { 2 } ] println
dict "k" { 1 } set println

// **It converts by its length.** Two terms: 2, 2.0, true — and a LIST of the
// body.
{ 1 2 } convert.to_int println
{ 1 2 } convert.to_float println
{ 1 2 } convert.to_bool println
lambda convert.to_bool println
{ 1 2 } convert.to_list println

// **A member that will not convert is left out, not reported.** A PAIR, a
// CLASS and a TIME each refuse on their own; as a dict's member each simply
// does not appear, key and all.
dict "k" 1 2 pair set println
dict "k" class set println
dict "a" 1 set "k" 5 time.timestamp set println
